Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)
🎯 Aim

To find the length of the longest subsequence in a given array such that:

The elements are strictly increasing

The elements are not required to be contiguous

🔍 Problem Statement

Given an integer array nums, return the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.

Example
Input:  nums = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]
Output: 4


Explanation:
The LIS is [2, 5, 7, 101]

🧠 Approach Used

Dynamic Programming (O(n²))
This is the most commonly expected approach in:

GATE

Placements

Semester exams

⚙️ Algorithm

Create a DP array dp[] where
dp[i] = length of LIS ending at index i

Initialize every dp[i] = 1 (each element is a subsequence by itself)

For every i, compare with all previous elements j < i

If nums[j] < nums[i], update:

dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)


The maximum value in dp[] is the answer

💻 Java Code (Clean & Interview-Ready)
class LongestIncreasingSubsequence {
    public static int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;

        // Step 1: Create DP array
        int[] dp = new int[n];

        // Step 2: Initialize all values to 1
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;
        }

        // Step 3: Build dp array
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 4: Find maximum value in dp
        int maxLIS = 0;
        for (int val : dp) {
            maxLIS = Math.max(maxLIS, val);
        }

        return maxLIS;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18};
        System.out.println(lengthOfLIS(nums));
    }
}

🧾 Output
4

🪜 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 int n = nums.length;

Stores the size of the array.

🔹 int[] dp = new int[n];

Creates a DP array where:

dp[i] stores LIS length ending at index i

🔹 Initialization Loop
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i] = 1;
}


Each element alone is an LIS of length 1.

🔹 Main DP Logic
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {
            dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
        }
    }
}


Compare current element nums[i] with all previous elements

If increasing order is maintained:

Extend the subsequence

Update dp[i] with the best possible value

🔹 Finding Final Answer
int maxLIS = 0;
for (int val : dp) {
    maxLIS = Math.max(maxLIS, val);
}


The longest LIS may end at any index, so we return the maximum.

⏱️ Time & Space Complexity

Time: O(n²)

Space: O(n)
